  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Platizio Marketing Site\n\nContext first: this is a fully static Next.js/React marketing site (no database, no `fetch`, no API routes — confirmed via search). So classic **N+1 query patterns don't apply here**; I've substituted the closest real analogue (redundant per-item work in render loops) under that heading. The real opportunities are in React re-renders, WebGL/animation cost, and redundant per-render computation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. \"N+1\"-equivalent: repeated work in list renders\n\nNo DB/API calls exist, but there are two spots doing non-trivial work *per item, per render* instead of once:\n\n**`src/components/sections/Trust.tsx`** — `AmfiSeal`/`SebiSeal` call `ticks(28,...)`/`ticks(8,...)` (trig math building `<line>` arrays) inside the component body, so it reruns on every render even though the output is 100% static.\n\n**`src/components/pages/AboutPage.tsx:82-97`** — `CompassSeal` recomputes 24 or 8 tick lines with `Math.cos`/`Math.sin` every render, for a component whose only prop (`variant`) has 2 fixed values.\n\nFix — hoist to module scope since there are only ever 2 variants:\n```tsx\n// outside the component, computed once at module load\nconst AMFI_TICKS = buildTicks(24, 23.5);\nconst SEBI_TICKS = buildTicks(8, 17);\n```\nContrast with `src/components/sections/CTA.tsx:8-16`, which already does this correctly (`TICKS` computed at module scope) — that's the pattern to replicate in `Trust.tsx` and `AboutPage.tsx`.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unnecessary re-renders\n\n**`src/components/Preloader.tsx:33-38`** — the biggest one. The load animation calls `setProgress(...)` inside `requestAnimationFrame`, so React re-renders the whole `Preloader` tree ~90 times over the 1.5s load (once per frame). Compare with `src/components/sections/Hero.tsx`'s `GrowthStat` (lines 57-81), which animates an equivalent numeric counter using `useMotionValue`/`useTransform` — **zero React re-renders**, all off the render cycle. Preloader should use the same pattern:\n```tsx\nconst progress = useMotionValue(0);\nconst label = useTransform(progress, (v) => Math.round(v).toString().padStart(3, \"0\"));\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const controls = animate(progress, 100, { duration: LOAD_MS / 1000, ease: \"easeOut\", onComplete: onDone });\n  return () => controls.stop();\n}, []);\n// render: <motion.span>{label}</motion.span>\n```\n\n**`src/components/sections/Products.tsx:197-245`** — `Products` holds `activeIndex`/`previewIndex` state. `ProductRow` is not memoized, and the callbacks passed to it (`onActivate={() => activate(i)}`, `onDeactivate={() => setActiveIndex(null)}`) are new closures every render. Hovering *any* one row re-renders **all 5** `ProductRow` instances. Fix:\n```tsx\nconst ProductRow = React.memo(function ProductRow({ ... }) { ... });\n// in parent, memoize with useCallback keyed by index, or pass index + one stable handler:\nconst handleActivate = useCallback((i: number) => { setActiveIndex(i); setPreviewIndex(i); }, []);\n```\n\n**`src/components/sections/Journey.tsx:61-65`** — `useMotionValueEvent(scrollYProgress, \"change\", ...)` calls `setActive` on *every* scroll-driven change tick. React bails out for same-value updates, but the whole `Journey` component (including the static heading `RevealWords`, which re-splits `text.split(\" \")` every render — see #5) re-renders each time `active` actually changes. Not severe, but worth noting since it's on a component with heavy motion/scroll children.\n\n**`src/components/Nav.tsx:25-30`** — scroll handler fires `setScrolled(...)` unthrottled on every native `scroll` event (which can fire far more often than once per frame on some browsers/trackpads). React bails on identical values, but the handler itself still runs every event. Throttle via rAF:\n```tsx\nuseEffect(() => {\n  let raf = 0;\n  const onScroll = () => {\n    if (raf) return;\n    raf = requestAnimationFrame(() => { setScrolled(window.scrollY > 48); raf = 0; });\n  };\n  ...\n}, []);\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Caching opportunities\n\n- **`src/components/sections/Trust.tsx` / `AboutPage.tsx` seals** — as above, these are pure functions of a 2-valued prop; hoisting to constants is effectively free memoization.\n- **`src/components/ui/Reveal.tsx:52`** (`RevealWords`) — `text.split(\" \")` runs on every render of every usage (there are ~10 call sites). Since `text` is static per call site in practice, wrap in `useMemo(() => text.split(\" \"), [text])`.\n- **Images** — `AboutPage.tsx` already uses `next/image` with `fill` + `sizes` correctly for both the founder photo and team grid (lines 259-265, 313-319) — good, no action needed, but confirm `next.config.ts` doesn't disable image optimization (worth a quick check if this hasn't been verified — it wasn't in scope of what was read).\n- **Fonts** — `next/font/google` in `layout.tsx` self-hosts and caches correctly already.\n- **`MarketConstellation`** is lazy-loaded via `next/dynamic(..., { ssr: false })` in `Hero.tsx:16-19` — correct pattern, keeps the ~3D dependency (`three`, `@react-three/fiber`) out of the initial bundle/critical path.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Memory leaks / uncontrolled background work\n\nNo classic leaks (all `useEffect` subscriptions have matching cleanup — scroll listeners, Lenis instance, pointer listeners, geometry disposal in `MarketConstellation.tsx:58`). The one real issue is **unbounded animation, not a leak**:\n\n**`src/components/three/MarketConstellation.tsx:179-189`** — `Canvas` runs `frameloop=\"always\"`, and `Terrain`'s `useFrame` (line 60-69) rewrites a `Float32Array` of 6,050 vertices (110×55) and re-uploads it to the GPU **every frame, forever**, even after the user has scrolled the hero out of view (there's no page-visibility or intersection-based pause). On a long scroll session this keeps burning CPU (typed-array rewrite) + GPU bandwidth (buffer re-upload) indefinitely on both `/` and effectively every session. Fix with an `IntersectionObserver` to flip `frameloop` to `\"demand\"` when the hero scrolls off-screen:\n```tsx\nconst [inView, setInView] = useState(true);\nuseEffect(() => {\n  const el = containerRef.current;\n  const io = new IntersectionObserver(([e]) => setInView(e.isIntersecting), { threshold: 0 });\n  io.observe(el);\n  return () => io.disconnect();\n}, []);\n// <Canvas frameloop={reduced || !inView ? \"demand\" : \"always\"} ...>\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Redundant computations\n\n- **`Reveal.tsx` `RevealWords`** (line 52) — `text.split(\" \")` on every render (see #3 fix, same memoization applies here as a redundant-computation fix).\n- **`Hero.tsx` `HeroLine`** (line 35) — same pattern, `words.split(\" \")` recomputed each render; low-impact here since Hero renders rarely, but the underlying anti-pattern is repeated across the codebase and should be fixed once in `Reveal.tsx`/wherever this split logic is centralized.\n- **`MarketConstellation.tsx` `waveY`** (lines 26-32, called from `useFrame` at line 60-69) — this is the single highest-impact redundant computation: **6,050 JS-side `sin`/`cos` evaluations every animation frame**, then a full buffer re-upload. This is necessary for the current CPU-driven approach, but the right fix is to move `waveY` into a GLSL vertex shader (`onBeforeCompile` or a custom `ShaderMaterial`) so the GPU computes displacement per-vertex in parallel, eliminating both the CPU trig loop and the per-frame `Float32Array`/`needsUpdate` upload entirely. This is the biggest win available in the codebase — it turns a CPU-bound per-frame cost proportional to vertex count into a fixed, GPU-parallel cost.\n\n---\n\n### Priority ranking\n\n| # | Fix | Effort | Impact |\n|---|-----|--------|--------|\n| 1 | `MarketConstellation`: pause `frameloop` when hero off-screen | Low | High (stops indefinite CPU/GPU burn) |\n| 2 | `MarketConstellation`: move `waveY` to a vertex shader | Medium | High (removes 6k trig calls + buffer upload per frame) |\n| 3 | `Preloader`: switch to `useMotionValue` like `GrowthStat` | Low | Medium (removes ~90 re-renders per page load) |\n| 4 | `Products.tsx`: memoize `ProductRow` + stabilize callbacks | Low | Medium (stops 5x re-render fanout on hover) |\n| 5 | Hoist `Trust`/`AboutPage` seal tick math to module constants | Low | Low-Medium |\n| 6 | Memoize `text.split(\" \")` in `Reveal.tsx`/`HeroLine` | Low | Low |\n| 7 | Throttle `Nav.tsx` scroll handler via rAF | Low | Low |\n\nWant me to implement any of these directly?\n",
